PlayableContent.put("game","zelda",{"store":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-switch/","verb":"explore, fight, solve; find the item hidden inside a dungeon, then use it to reach the boss, and the next dungeon","first30":"The Legend of Zelda (1986) sets the formula in its first thirty seconds: the game drops the player straight onto an open Hyrule overworld screen with no tutorial, a cave mouth visible nearby. Stepping inside, an old man says only “It’s dangerous to go alone! Take this,” and hands over a sword with no further explanation. Walking back out into the overworld, every direction is equally open and equally unexplained: the only clue to where the first dungeon lies is whatever the player is willing to walk into.","minute":"Push toward the dungeon whose entrance you can already see, or spend a few more minutes bombing suspicious walls and burning suspicious bushes for the rupees, hearts and secrets a sword slash cannot reveal? Fight through this room with the tools already in hand, or retreat and come back once the item two rooms ahead is finally yours? Every entry keeps asking some version of the same question: is this obstacle solvable now, or does it mark where to return once you are somebody else?","engines":["discovery","mastery","progression","power"],"why":"For three decades the series ran on one closed loop: a dungeon hides an item behind its own puzzles, that item is also the key to the dungeon’s last door and often to some obstacle blocking the wider overworld, and finding it turns a wall the player already met into a route. Nintendo kept that loop legible across completely different hardware, from the Famicom Disk System to the Nintendo Switch, by tying every new tool to a single, telegraphed weakness (a locked door shaped like the item, a switch only a boomerang or hookshot can hit) rather than to invisible stats. Ocarina of Time (1998) proved the loop survived the jump to 3D once Z-targeting solved free-camera combat, and every entry through Skyward Sword (2011) kept refining the same dungeon-item shape. Breath of the Wild (2017) then broke the oldest half of that loop on purpose, handing Link every core rune before the first real dungeon and asking the player to solve the world with a fixed toolkit instead of a growing one, a structural shift Nintendo has kept rather than reversed in Tears of the Kingdom (2023).","complaints":"The loop’s companion characters have repeatedly drawn complaints for over-explaining it: Navi’s “Hey! Listen!” in Ocarina of Time and Fi’s constant interjections in Skyward Sword were both singled out by reviewers and players as hand-holding that slowed a system players could otherwise read from the room itself. Traversal has its own recurring complaint: The Wind Waker’s open Great Sea asked for long stretches of sailing between islands, and Skyward Sword’s Loftwing flight and repeated surface regions drew similar fatigue complaints. Skyward Sword’s Wii MotionPlus swordplay was also called unreliable by several reviewers, a friction Breath of the Wild and Tears of the Kingdom removed by returning sword-swinging to a button.","lesson":"A dungeon-and-item structure stays interesting for decades if every new tool is tied to a specific, telegraphed weakness rather than a stat increase, so a player can reason “this needs the item I do not have yet” instead of grinding for one. That same structure can still calcify: by Skyward Sword, the formula was drawing complaints of its own, its linearity above all, which is what let Breath of the Wild’s harder bet, removing the lock itself, read as a renewal rather than a gimmick. Keep the parts that teach and gate fairly; be willing to remove the part that has become simply familiar.","misses":"Copies of the dungeon-item loop often hand out new abilities that are simply stronger than the last, rather than each one answering one specific, previously-seen obstacle, so backtracking never pays off with the “of course” feeling the series is built on. Copies of Breath of the Wild’s open structure just as often borrow the empty map and the climbing button without building Nintendo’s chemistry engine underneath it, the physics and elemental interactions that make an empty-looking hillside worth climbing in the first place; an open world with nothing for its systems to combine into is just a very large dungeon with the doors removed.","entries":[{"t":"The Legend of Zelda","short":"Formula set","platform":"Famicom Disk System, NES","added":"Introduced the open Hyrule overworld, dungeons that hide the item needed to finish them, the Triforce, and one of the first console games with its own battery save.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-nes.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda (Wii U Virtual Console release of the 1986 game): Link fights Octoroks on an overworld screen beside a lake and wooden dock, the map, item slots and hearts above.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Games/NES/The-Legend-of-Zelda-796345.html","licence":"press","source":"Nintendo UK"}}},{"t":"Zelda II: The Adventure of Link","short":"Side-scroll break","platform":"Famicom Disk System, NES","added":"Replaced overworld combat with side-scrolling platforming, added experience-point levels and a lives-and-continues system the series then dropped from every later entry.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-aol.webp","alt":"Zelda II: The Adventure of Link (Wii U Virtual Console release of the 1987 game): Link leaps at a red palace guard in a side-scrolling palace hall, magic and life meters and the experience counter above.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Games/NES/Zelda-II-The-Adventure-of-Link-823290.html","licence":"press","source":"Nintendo UK"}}},{"t":"A Link to the Past","short":"Light and Dark Worlds","platform":"Super Nintendo Entertainment System","added":"Added a second, corrupted Dark World mirroring Hyrule’s Light World, so dungeons and items in one world unlock progress in the other, refining the template every later 2D entry reused.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-lttp-new.webp","alt":"A Link to the Past (1991, Super Nintendo): Link stands on the Master Sword’s pedestal in a forest clearing, with the magic meter, item box, rupee, bomb and arrow counters and hearts along the top.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/images/1281-the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-to-the-past","licence":"capture","source":"LaunchBox Games Database"}}},{"t":"Link's Awakening","short":"Handheld Zelda","platform":"Game Boy","added":"Moved the series to a handheld and away from Hyrule: the story takes place on Koholint Island, with no Princess Zelda and no Triforce, and a cast of unusual townspeople. It sold 3.83 million copies by 2004, plus 2.22 million of the colour DX version.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-la.webp","alt":"Link’s Awakening (1993, Game Boy): Link stands in a tiled dungeon room between two stone statues, a spiky creature at the top, with item, rupee and heart counters along the bottom.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/images/3033-the-legend-of-zelda-links-awakening","licence":"capture","source":"LaunchBox Games Database"}}},{"t":"Ocarina of Time","short":"3D template","platform":"Nintendo 64","added":"Carried the dungeon-item loop into 3D using Z-targeting to solve free-camera combat, becoming the first game to score a perfect 40 out of 40 in Famitsu.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e4.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (2026 Nintendo Switch 2 remake): Link walks down a misty path through Kokiri Forest, its lit tree houses ahead.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-time-switch-2/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Majora's Mask","short":"Three-day loop","platform":"Nintendo 64","added":"Reused Ocarina of Time’s engine and character models so a smaller team could finish in about 15 months, and built the game around a three-day cycle the player rewinds with the Song of Time, keeping items but resetting most quests. It sold 3.36 million copies.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-mm.webp","alt":"Majora’s Mask (2000): Deku Link faces a targeted enemy in a purple cave, with hearts and the magic bar top left, the “2nd” day dial and the map at the bottom.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/images/167-the-legend-of-zelda-majoras-mask","licence":"capture","source":"LaunchBox Games Database"}}},{"t":"The Wind Waker","short":"Cel-shaded Great Sea","platform":"GameCube","added":"Replaced Hyrule’s overworld with a sailable Great Sea and a cel-shaded cartoon art style that divided fans and critics the moment it was revealed at Spaceworld 2001.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-windwaker.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker, shown in its Wind Waker HD remaster (Wii U, 2013): Link sails the King of Red Lions across the moonlit Great Sea, the wind-direction compass and sea chart at bottom left.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Games/Wii-U-games/The-Legend-of-Zelda-The-Wind-Waker-HD-765386.html","licence":"press","source":"Nintendo UK"}}},{"t":"Twilight Princess","short":"Realism, reversed","platform":"GameCube, Wii","added":"Answered the Wind Waker backlash with a deliberately darker, more realistic Hyrule, and launched on Wii with motion controls in November 2006, followed weeks later by a GameCube version, Nintendo’s last game for that console.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-tp.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Twilight Princess (2006, Wii): Link in wolf form, with Midna riding on his back, pads through an overgrown wooden enclosure.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Games/Wii/The-Legend-of-Zelda-Twilight-Princess-283542.html","licence":"press","source":"Nintendo UK"}}},{"t":"Skyward Sword","short":"Motion swordplay","platform":"Wii","added":"Built combat entirely around Wii MotionPlus one-to-one sword swings inside a story critics faulted for its linearity, guided by the companion Fi, whose frequent interjections drew mostly negative reactions.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e7.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Skyward Sword HD (2021 remaster): Link strikes a chained, sealed door, sparks bursting where his blade meets its lock.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-skyward-sword-hd-switch/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"A Link Between Worlds","short":"Rented items","platform":"Nintendo 3DS","added":"Let players rent or buy every item from a shop run by Ravio instead of finding them in dungeons, which lets most dungeons be done in any order. Rented items go back to the shop if Link dies. It holds 91 on Metacritic and sold 4.26 million copies by 2022.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e-albw.webp","alt":"A Link Between Worlds (2013): Link stands outside his house beside a signpost and a patch of bushes on the 3DS’s top screen, three hearts at bottom left.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/images/17596-the-legend-of-zelda-a-link-between-worlds","licence":"capture","source":"LaunchBox Games Database"}}},{"t":"Breath of the Wild","short":"Open-air break","platform":"Wii U, Nintendo Switch","added":"Removed the dungeon-item lock entirely: every core rune arrives on the first plateau, and 120 shrines plus four Divine Beasts can be tackled in any order at all.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e8.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild (2017): Link, sword drawn, squares up to a red-clad Yiga Clan warrior in a dim, lantern-lit hall.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-switch/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Tears of the Kingdom","short":"Same Hyrule, built up","platform":"Nintendo Switch","added":"Kept Breath of the Wild’s Hyrule landmass almost unchanged rather than redrawing it, and added floating sky islands, an underground Depths, and Ultrahand building in place of new runes.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-e9.webp","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom (2023): Link drives a Zonai construct vehicle he built across an open Hyrule field, distant mountains behind him.","credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-switch/","licence":"store"}}}],"constant":"Nearly every mainline entry, from the 1986 original through Tears of the Kingdom, keeps a young, mostly silent hero named Link and a Zelda, usually a princess, and most set a villain, Ganon or Ganondorf, after the Triforce of Power, though Majora’s Mask and Skyward Sword pit Link against other foes. Progress still runs on finding a tool, usually inside a dungeon guarded by a boss, that the world outside was withholding until that point; even Breath of the Wild, which hands over its core runes at once, still gates its four Divine Beasts and final fight behind exploration, hearts and preparation rather than a straight line. Most entries are set in a kingdom called Hyrule, and the three-triangle Triforce myth recurs even when the plot barely mentions it along the way.","changed":"The series renewed its own structure twice. Ocarina of Time (1998) solved the specific problem of moving the 2D dungeon-item loop into 3D: Z-targeting let the camera lock onto an enemy or object so combat and item puzzles stayed readable in three dimensions, and every entry through Skyward Sword (2011) kept that solution largely intact, differing mostly in art, controls and how much story it carried. Breath of the Wild (2017) broke the older half of the formula instead: it handed Link the paraglider and his Magnesis, Stasis, Cryonis and Remote Bombs runes on the very first plateau, before any real dungeon, then let 120 shrines and four Divine Beasts be tackled in any order, with no single item found in one place gating access to another. Tears of the Kingdom (2023) kept that open structure rather than renewing it a third time, adding vertical zones and building tools to the same unlocked Hyrule instead of a new world or a new lock.","reception":[{"entry":"Zelda II: The Adventure of Link","year":1987,"verdict":"mixed","evidence":"Famicom Tsūshin scored it 36 out of 40 on release and Nintendo Power named it Game of the Year for 1988, but it sold 4.38 million copies, about two million fewer than the 1986 original, and later coverage cooled: GameRankings averaged its NES reviews at 78 per cent, IGN told players not to expect classic Zelda gameplay, and GameSpot in 2007 cited its “questionable design decision[s]”.","why":"Where the 1986 original was entirely top-down, Zelda II replaced overworld combat with side-scrolling platforming, added an experience-point levelling system for attack, magic and life, and introduced a lives-and-continues structure no later Zelda kept. Reviewers of the time judged the experiment on its own considerable ambition and rewarded it; Nintendo itself judged it differently, building every subsequent entry on the 1986 template instead of this one, which is why later coverage frames it as the series’ one rejected direction rather than a foundation.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zelda_II:_The_Adventure_of_Link"]},{"entry":"Ocarina of Time","year":1998,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"Famitsu awarded it a 40 out of 40, the magazine’s first perfect score; it holds a 99 out of 100 on Metacritic, the highest score of any game on that aggregator; and it sold over 500,000 US pre-orders, over a million US copies in under a week, and 7.6 million copies worldwide on Nintendo 64.","why":"Ocarina of Time did not reinvent the dungeon-item loop; it solved the one problem standing between that loop and full 3D, a free camera that could track combat and puzzle targets without confusing the player, through Z-targeting. Reviewers rewarded a game that felt completely new in dimension while staying legible in structure, a combination whose sales neither of the next two home-console entries, Majora’s Mask and The Wind Waker, went on to match.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time","https://www.metacritic.com/game/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-time/critic-reviews/"]},{"entry":"Majora’s Mask","year":2000,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"Majora’s Mask holds 95 out of 100 on Metacritic from 27 reviews and sold 3.36 million copies on Nintendo 64; IGN gave it 9.9 out of 10, while GameSpot’s Jeff Gerstmann gave it 8.3, frustrated by its time pressure and heavier focus on side quests.","why":"The team built the game in 15 months by reusing assets from Ocarina of Time, and made a three-day cycle the player resets the centre of it. Critics likened the loop to Groundhog Day and praised the Bombers’ Notebook, the 24 masks and the dense town of Termina. Some found the repeating clock aggravating, and the ageing hardware showed in frame-rate drops and blurry textures. The time limit that annoyed some players was also what critics called the game’s best idea.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Majora%27s_Mask"]},{"entry":"The Wind Waker","year":2002,"verdict":"mixed","evidence":"Critics gave it universal acclaim, 96 out of 100 on Metacritic with perfect scores from Famitsu, Game Informer and Nintendo Power, yet it sold 4.6 million copies, far below Ocarina of Time’s 7.6 million; director Eiji Aonuma called purchases disappointing in both Japan and North America, and Nintendo of America blamed the weak North American sales largely on the cel-shaded art.","why":"Wind Waker changed something nobody had asked it to change: after Nintendo had shown a realistic Zelda tech demo at Spaceworld 2000, revealing a cartoon, cel-shaded Link at Spaceworld 2001 read to much of the fanbase as a broken promise rather than a fresh choice, and the game was mocked as “Celda” before release. Universal critical acclaim could not offset a sales shortfall that Nintendo of America blamed on the art style, and the game’s standing only recovered once enough time had passed for critics to judge the style on its own terms.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_The_Wind_Waker"]},{"entry":"Twilight Princess","year":2006,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"It scored 96 out of 100 on Metacritic for GameCube and 95 for Wii, behind only Ocarina of Time’s 99 among Zelda games and level with The Wind Waker’s 96, with perfect scores from outlets including 1UP.com, EGM and GameSpy; it also holds the Guinness World Record for most critically acclaimed action-adventure game on Wii.","why":"Where Wind Waker had traded realism for cartoon style, Twilight Princess reversed course on purpose: Aonuma has said the team “did a 180, thinking ‘maybe this is what the fans want’” after the Wind Waker backlash, building a deliberately darker, more mature-looking Hyrule and launching it as the Wii’s motion-control showcase alongside a parallel GameCube version for players without the new hardware. Players and critics rewarded the reversal with near-top aggregate scores and 8.85 million sales across both consoles, almost double The Wind Waker’s, even as some faulted blurry Wii textures and, in GameSpot’s words, motion controls that felt “tacked-on”.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Twilight_Princess","https://zeldauniverse.net/2013/10/16/aonuma-wind-wakers-reversal-in-receptiontwilight-princess-visuals-influenced-by-fans-tested-twilight-princess-in-hd/"]},{"entry":"Skyward Sword","year":2011,"verdict":"mixed","evidence":"It scored 93 out of 100 on Metacritic at launch, with perfect scores from IGN, Eurogamer, Game Informer and Edge and four perfect 10s from Famitsu, yet it sold 3.67 million copies, under half of Twilight Princess’s 8.85 million; retrospective opinion turned far more mixed over its linearity and Fi’s interjections, and the 2021 HD remaster, which added a button-based control option, scored 81.","why":"Skyward Sword built its whole combat system around Wii MotionPlus one-to-one sword swings and wrapped it in arguably the series’ most linear, most heavily tutorialised structure, with the companion Fi interjecting to explain systems the room could otherwise teach. Critics praised the precision at launch, but Breath of the Wild’s developers later said they built a non-linear world in response to player complaints about Skyward Sword’s linearity, and the HD remaster’s lower score suggests the design aged less well than its first reviews.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Skyward_Sword"]},{"entry":"A Link Between Worlds","year":2013,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"A Link Between Worlds holds 91 out of 100 on Metacritic, the fourth highest of any 3DS game; Game Informer gave it 10 out of 10, IGN 9.4, GameSpot 9 and Polygon 9.5. It sold over 2.5 million copies in five months and 4.26 million by 2022.","why":"Players rent or buy items from the merchant Ravio instead of finding them in dungeons, so most dungeons can be taken in any order. Link can also flatten into a 2D mural to cross walls and travel between Hyrule and Lorule. Polygon called it the best Zelda game in 20 years, and critics praised the wall-merging puzzles and the soundtrack. The art style was the most divisive part.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_A_Link_Between_Worlds"]},{"entry":"Breath of the Wild","year":2017,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"It scored 97 out of 100 on Metacritic for Switch, sold 35.76 million copies across Switch and Wii U by June 2026, the most of any Zelda game, and won Game of the Year at The Game Awards 2017, the 2018 D.I.C.E. Awards and the 2018 Game Developers Choice Awards; at the 2018 BAFTA Games Awards it won Game Innovation, while Best Game went to What Remains of Edith Finch.","why":"Breath of the Wild reversed nearly everything reviewers had used against Skyward Sword: instead of a linear story and hand-holding, it opened Hyrule from the first plateau with almost no required order; instead of a growing toolkit gating a fixed number of dungeons, every core rune arrived at once and 120 shrines plus four Divine Beasts could be solved in any sequence. It is the single largest structural change in the series’ history, and critics and the industry’s own award juries rewarded it as such.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Game_Awards_2017","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D.I.C.E._Award_for_Game_of_the_Year","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_Developers_Choice_Award_for_Game_of_the_Year","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14th_British_Academy_Games_Awards"]},{"entry":"Tears of the Kingdom","year":2023,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"It scored 96 out of 100 on Metacritic for Switch, sold over 10 million copies in its first three days, the fastest-selling Zelda game, and reached 22.71 million copies by June 2026; at The Game Awards 2023 it won Best Action/Adventure Game, though Baldur’s Gate 3 won the overall Game of the Year that year.","why":"Rather than break its own formula a third time, Tears of the Kingdom kept Breath of the Wild’s Hyrule landmass almost unchanged and built vertically instead, adding sky islands above and a Depths layer below, and replaced new runes with Ultrahand, Fuse and Autobuild, tools for building rather than unlocking. Critics scored it almost identically to its predecessor, praising the new construction systems, and it became the fastest-selling Zelda game even though most of its surface was ground Breath of the Wild players already knew.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Tears_of_the_Kingdom","https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2023/12/zelda-tears-of-the-kingdom-wins-best-actionadventure-game-at-tga-2023"]}],"receptionLesson":"Across these nine entries, the deciding factor was not how much an entry changed but what it chose to change. Twilight Princess and Breath of the Wild each reversed a specific, nameable complaint from the entry right before them, Wind Waker’s cartoon art and Skyward Sword’s linearity and hand-holding, and both were rewarded, Twilight Princess with sales nearly double The Wind Waker’s and Breath of the Wild with the series’ best sales and three Game of the Year awards. Wind Waker’s own backlash came from changing something nobody had complained about: Nintendo’s own realistic Spaceworld 2000 tech demo had set an expectation, so trading it for cel-shading felt forced on players rather than requested by them. A designer inheriting a beloved format should identify which specific complaint the last entry actually earned, from players and from the developers’ own read of reviews, and spend the next entry’s one big swing there, rather than on whichever change looks most dramatic on a trailer.","diagrams":[{"title":"The Legend of Zelda: the entries that shaped it","steps":[{"id":"e0","t":"Formula set","d":"1986 · Famicom Disk System, NES"},{"id":"e1","t":"Side-scroll break","d":"1987 · Famicom Disk System, NES"},{"id":"e2","t":"Light and Dark Worlds","d":"1991 · Super Nintendo Entertainment System"},{"id":"e3","t":"Handheld Zelda","d":"1993 · Game Boy"},{"id":"e4","t":"3D template","d":"1998 · Nintendo 64"},{"id":"e5","t":"Three-day loop","d":"2000 · Nintendo 64"},{"id":"e6","t":"Cel-shaded Great Sea","d":"2002 · GameCube"},{"id":"e7","t":"Realism, reversed","d":"2006 · GameCube, Wii"},{"id":"e8","t":"Motion swordplay","d":"2011 · Wii"},{"id":"e9","t":"Rented items","d":"2013 · Nintendo 3DS"},{"id":"e10","t":"Open-air break","d":"2017 · Wii U, Nintendo Switch"},{"id":"e11","t":"Same Hyrule, built up","d":"2023 · Nintendo Switch"}],"edges":[["e0","e1"],["e1","e2"],["e2","e3"],["e3","e4"],["e4","e5"],["e5","e6"],["e6","e7"],["e7","e8"],["e8","e9"],["e9","e10"],["e10","e11"]]},{"title":"Zelda: solve a dungeon, open the world","steps":[{"t":"Explore Hyrule","d":"find a dungeon entrance"},{"t":"Solve the dungeon","d":"fight and puzzle toward the item"},{"t":"Win the item","d":"beat the boss guarding it"},{"t":"Reach further","d":"the item opens routes outside"}]}],"signature":{"mechanism":"For most of the series, each dungeon is a self-contained puzzle box built around one new item, a boomerang, a hookshot, a bow, that both finishes that same dungeon and clears a specific, previously-seen obstacle somewhere in the wider overworld, so progress is a chain of doors each opened by the last one solved. Breath of the Wild (2017) rewired this: Link can climb from the start and receives the paraglider and his Magnesis, Stasis, Cryonis and Remote Bombs runes on the Great Plateau before any real dungeon, and afterwards the world gates progress by preparation, hearts, weapon quality, cold or heat resistance, rather than by which single verb the player happens to own yet.","teach":"The 1986 original teaches the whole shape with one line and one room: an old man says “It’s dangerous to go alone! Take this,” handing over a sword with no menu to read, and the very first dungeon usually shows a locked door or a gap the player cannot yet cross, planting a question the item found deeper in that same dungeon answers a few rooms later. Later entries keep the device even as the fiction grows: Ocarina of Time’s first dungeon, the Great Deku Tree, still ends with a boss battle that only the item just found inside can realistically win, so the lesson is rehearsed before the overworld ever tests it.","fair":"A wrong guess in this loop costs time, not the run: a locked door does not explode if you attack it with the wrong tool, and since 1986 most dungeons have hidden their own map and compass, which reveal the layout and where the treasure and boss lie, so a lost player has a floor to fall back on. Companion characters, from the fairy Navi in Ocarina of Time to Fi in Skyward Sword, extend that same safety net into direct hints, though both were criticised for handing out that help before players had asked for it, which is one complaint Breath of the Wild answered by removing a talking companion almost entirely.","escalate":"Escalation runs on two clocks at once. Inside a single dungeon, later rooms usually demand the item just found in combination with an older one, so the dungeon’s own difficulty curve is really a combination lock the player is assembling in real time. Across the series, Nintendo escalated the whole loop’s scale rather than its logic: eight-bit overworld screens in 1986 grew into A Link to the Past’s two-world map in 1991, then into Ocarina of Time’s continuous 3D Hyrule Field in 1998, and finally into Breath of the Wild’s single, physically simulated landmass in 2017, where the escalation is how much of that world a player can reach on day one rather than how many items stand between them and it.","copies":"Games that copy the item-gate shape often hand out new abilities that are simply stronger than the last one, rather than each new tool answering one specific, already-seen obstacle, so backtracking never produces the “of course” realisation the format depends on; a metroidvania that gates a route behind an upgrade nobody has previously seen block anything is asking players to remember a promise the level never actually made. Open-world games that copy Breath of the Wild’s all-at-once toolkit run the opposite risk: without a chemistry engine underneath, letting fire spread through grass, metal draw lightning, wood float, an empty map with a climbing button is simply a very large dungeon with every door removed and nothing put in their place.","prototype":"Grey-box three connected rooms and one small overworld area. Put a locked or oversized obstacle in room one that is clearly solvable only by a tool found in room two, and put a second, previously-unreachable obstacle in the overworld that the same tool clears. Watch whether a first-time player returns to the overworld obstacle unprompted once they have the tool, without being told to; that unprompted return is the whole loop working. Then try removing the gate for one tool entirely, giving it up front, and watch whether the same obstacle still gets solved for its own sake or only ever in passing."},"lens":{"gameplay":{"claim":"Breath of the Wild (2017) is the one mainline Zelda to remove the series’ oldest rule, that a specific item found inside a dungeon is required to solve a specific obstacle elsewhere, by handing Link every core rune before any real dungeon exists.","evidence":"From 1986 through Skyward Sword (2011), items such as the boomerang, hookshot and bow were each found inside one dungeon and required to finish that dungeon or clear a named overworld obstacle. Breath of the Wild instead gives Link the Magnesis, Stasis, Cryonis and Remote Bombs runes, plus the paraglider, on the Great Plateau, before he reaches Hyrule proper or any of the four Divine Beasts, tackled in any order.","mechanism":"Removing the item-gate does not remove challenge; it moves the challenge from “which item do I own” to “how prepared am I”, using a shared physics and elemental chemistry engine (fire spreads through grass, metal draws lightning, wood floats) so most obstacles have more than one valid solution once every tool is already in hand.","effect":"A player can walk toward Hyrule Castle, the game’s hardest single target, within the first hour, and either die quickly or improvise a way past it; the freedom to fail early, rather than being mechanically blocked, is itself new information about how hard the game plans to be.","compare":"Elden Ring (2022), whose director Hidetaka Miyazaki has cited Breath of the Wild as a design influence, adopts a similar all-at-once structure for a different genre: its Lands Between can be crossed in almost any direction from the opening hour, with difficulty managed by preparation and level rather than a required item sequence.","cost":"Every shrine and Divine Beast must be solvable by a player who could, in principle, be missing nothing or almost everything, so no puzzle can assume a specific late-game tool the way A Link to the Past’s later dungeons assumed the hookshot; Nintendo pays with more general, less item-specific puzzle design.","principle":"Removing a series’ oldest gating mechanism only stays fair if every remaining test can already be solved by whatever a player happens to be carrying at the time; design the puzzles around a shared toolkit’s interactions, not around the assumption that a specific tool has already been found.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_A_Link_to_the_Past","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elden_Ring"]},"ui":{"claim":"Breath of the Wild (2017) turns one of its menus into a camera: a creature, weapon or material enters the Hyrule Compendium only after Link raises the Sheikah Slate, an in-fiction tablet, and photographs it in the world.","evidence":"The 1986 original manages items through a pause-screen grid with no in-world equivalent, and Ocarina of Time (1998) keeps a similar abstract subscreen while mapping items to its C buttons. Breath of the Wild gives Link the Sheikah Slate on the Great Plateau and later a camera function; its Hyrule Compendium, a catalogue of creatures, monsters, materials and equipment, fills only as the player takes a picture of each one, and Tears of the Kingdom (2023) keeps the same rule on the Purah Pad.","mechanism":"In Fagerholt and Lorentzon’s terms the camera view is diegetic, Link visibly raising the Slate inside the world, while the compendium it fills is a meta interface: a flat screen that exists in the fiction as the Slate’s contents but not in the 3D space. The diegetic act decides what the meta screen may contain.","effect":"A bestiary stops being a list the game fills in for the player and becomes a record of where they went and what they stopped to look at: an empty entry is a creature nobody photographed, so completing the catalogue sends the player back into the world rather than through a menu.","compare":"Pokémon Red and Blue (1996) complete their Pokédex by capturing, evolving and trading all 151 creatures, so the catalogue records what the player owns; Breath of the Wild’s compendium records only what the player stopped, framed and photographed, turning collection into a photography task.","cost":"A creature that flees or a boss fought once can leave a hole in the catalogue that no amount of skill fixes later, so a photo-based catalogue needs a planned way to fill missed entries, or completionists end up punished for a moment they did not know mattered.","principle":"When a menu catalogues the world, make filling it an act performed in the world: a catalogue that records only what the player went and looked at turns completion into exploration rather than acquisition.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time"]},"art":{"claim":"By its own director’s account, Nintendo swung Zelda’s art direction between cartoon and realism in response to audience reaction, reversing The Wind Waker’s cel-shading with Twilight Princess’s deliberate realism.","evidence":"The Wind Waker (2002) revealed cel-shaded, cartoon visuals at Spaceworld 2001 after a realistic tech demo the year before had set different expectations, and critics derisively dubbed it “Celda”. Twilight Princess (2006) responded with a darker, more photorealistic Hyrule; director Eiji Aonuma has said the team “did a 180, thinking ‘maybe this is what the fans want’” after seeing the Wind Waker backlash.","mechanism":"Treating art style as a lever a publisher pulls in direct response to public reaction, rather than an aesthetic fixed early and defended, turns character and world design into a risk-management tool: a style that tests badly with a vocal audience segment can be reversed for the very next flagship entry rather than defended across a generation.","effect":"Long-time players experienced two consecutive HD-era Zelda entries with almost opposite visual identities, which let critics and players directly compare a cartoon Hyrule against a realistic one within a single console generation, something a franchise with one fixed style never offers.","compare":"Paramount’s 2019 Sonic the Hedgehog film underwent the same kind of reactive reversal on a much shorter timeline: the character’s first trailer drew immediate backlash over his human-like teeth and legs, and the studio delayed the film by three months specifically to redesign Sonic before a second trailer, trading a few months for a redesign the way Nintendo traded a full console generation.","cost":"Rebuilding an entire game’s visual identity in direct response to a single announcement’s reception risks alienating whichever audience preferred the discarded style, and Wind Waker HD’s later 2013 remaster, released after Twilight Princess, shows Nintendo eventually chose to preserve the cel-shaded original rather than repaint it, rather than treating the reversal as permanent doctrine.","principle":"A visible, public reaction to a reveal trailer is real design data, but weigh it against your own stated intent rather than treating it as an instruction, and scope any reversal to the entry that provoked it; Nintendo reversed course for Twilight Princess yet kept the cel-shaded look for the handheld sequel Phantom Hourglass (2007).","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_The_Wind_Waker","https://zeldauniverse.net/2013/10/16/aonuma-wind-wakers-reversal-in-receptiontwilight-princess-visuals-influenced-by-fans-tested-twilight-princess-in-hd/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_the_Hedgehog_(film)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Phantom_Hourglass"]},"sound":{"claim":"Several mainline entries make playing music a mechanic rather than only a score: Ocarina of Time’s ocarina songs, Wind Waker’s conductor’s baton and Skyward Sword’s harp are all diegetic instruments whose tunes open doors, warp across the map or change the weather.","evidence":"Ocarina of Time (1998) has Link learn and play 12 songs, including Zelda’s Lullaby and the Song of Storms, each triggering a specific effect such as opening a sealed door or warping to a temple. The Wind Waker (2002) replaces the ocarina with a conductor’s baton for six songs that control wind direction, warp between islands and change the time of day, and Skyward Sword (2011) has Link strum a harp for similarly functional tunes.","mechanism":"These songs sit across both fiction and interface at once: a nearby character can hear Link play, so the performance is diegetic, while the same input also fires a non-diegetic system effect, a warp, a weather change, with no in-world explanation for how a tune moves matter or opens ancient stone.","effect":"Learning a song becomes as memorable as learning an item, since the player must physically input a short melody to use it again, turning part of the score into something rehearsed and performed rather than only heard.","compare":"Most Final Fantasy scores use a signature theme, the Prelude or a character motif, purely as a non-diegetic emotional cue no character in the story can hear or trigger; Zelda’s playable songs give an equally memorable melody an actual function the player must supply on demand.","cost":"Tying game logic to a performed melody makes the input itself, recalling and re-playing an exact sequence on an ocarina or motion-controlled harp, a real skill test unrelated to combat or puzzle-solving; a player who cannot execute the song is blocked by a music exam, not the dungeon’s logic.","principle":"When a game’s fiction already gives the player an instrument, consider making its songs functional inputs rather than ambient flavour; asking players to physically perform a melody to trigger an effect makes that effect memorable in a way a menu command with the same result never is.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_The_Wind_Waker","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Skyward_Sword"]},"lore":{"claim":"Nintendo left its timeline officially unexplained for twenty-five years, then resolved the contradictions not inside a new game but in a 2011 reference book, Hyrule Historia, which split the chronology into three official branches.","evidence":"Hyrule Historia, published 21 December 2011 and a 276-page book credited to Nintendo with an afterword by producer Eiji Aonuma, sets out an official timeline explaining that the ending of Ocarina of Time splits the series’ history into three branches depending on whether and how Link defeats Ganon, reconciling decades of fan speculation about how earlier entries connected. The book itself qualifies the result: “this chronicle merely collects information that is believed to be true at this time.”","mechanism":"This is worldbuilding delivered through a paratext, material published alongside the games rather than inside any single one of them, letting Nintendo answer a question, how do all these Hyrules relate, that no individual entry’s script was ever built to carry.","effect":"Players who had spent over a decade building competing fan timelines from in-game clues received an answer that validated some theories and discarded others at a stroke, converting years of forum argument into settled, purchasable canon while the qualifying sentence quietly kept the door open for Nintendo to revise it later.","compare":"Lucasfilm took the opposite approach to a similarly tangled continuity in 2014, rebranding the entire pre-Disney Star Wars Expanded Universe as non-canonical “Legends” rather than reconciling it, erasing the contradictions instead of organising them.","cost":"Publishing an official timeline forecloses interpretations some fans preferred and hands Nintendo’s own later writers a reference they must either honour or publicly contradict, a constraint no game had explicitly bound itself to before 2011.","principle":"If a long-running series’ internal contradictions have already become common knowledge among its audience, consider organising them in a dedicated reference work rather than either ignoring them indefinitely or erasing them the way a rebrand does; a paratext can settle a continuity question no single entry’s plot has room to answer.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Hyrule_Historia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda"]},"world":{"claim":"Tears of the Kingdom (2023) reuses its direct predecessor’s Hyrule landmass instead of redrawing the kingdom; the only earlier precedent, A Link Between Worlds (2013), rebuilt A Link to the Past’s map 22 years after it, not six.","evidence":"Tears of the Kingdom’s surface world is explicitly the same map as Breath of the Wild’s Hyrule, modified with new chasms and story-driven changes rather than replaced. By contrast, Majora’s Mask (2000) reused Ocarina of Time’s engine and many character models but built an entirely new, geographically distinct land, Termina, in about a year of development.","mechanism":"Reusing the actual landmass, not just the engine or assets, changes what returning exploration means: a player is not learning a new map’s logic but re-reading a known one for what has specifically changed, which only works as a design choice if the changes themselves (chasms, sky islands, a Depths layer) are made the point rather than hidden as a cost-saving shortcut.","effect":"Because the surface was already known, the new content went onto other axes, sky islands above and the Depths below, so a returning player’s question shifts from which direction to explore to which layer, and the familiar surface becomes the hub between the two new ones.","compare":"Majora’s Mask solved the same schedule pressure, reuse what exists to ship fast, by keeping the engine and cast but inventing a new setting; Tears of the Kingdom solved it by keeping the setting itself and building new layers above and below it instead.","cost":"Keeping the same landmass means the game cannot offer the pure discovery of an unfamiliar map to players who finished Breath of the Wild, so all of the sense of newness has to come from the vertical additions and the changes layered onto ground players may already know by heart.","principle":"When a sequel keeps its predecessor’s map, put the new content on a different axis rather than scattering it across the old ground: a new layer above or below gives returning players a direction the old map never had.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Tears_of_the_Kingdom","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Majora%27s_Mask","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_A_Link_Between_Worlds"]},"env":{"claim":"Breath of the Wild’s hundred-year-old ruins carry a second layer of environmental storytelling invisible to a first-time player: a returning series veteran can recognise a specific pile of rubble as a place they once walked through intact in an earlier Hyrule.","evidence":"Breath of the Wild (2017) stages Hyrule Castle Town and the Temple of Time as decayed rubble a hundred years after the Great Calamity, with no dialogue explaining most individual ruins; both carry the names of places players walked through intact in earlier entries such as Ocarina of Time and Twilight Princess.","mechanism":"Ordinary environmental storytelling asks any player to infer a past from present staging alone; a long-running series can additionally reward a specific returning audience with a second, comparative read, recognising what a ruin used to be because they have personally stood in its intact version, a resource only a franchise with real history can spend.","effect":"A first-time player reads the ruins as generic evidence of catastrophe; a veteran player experiences a small, private moment of recognition and loss at the same rubble, without the game spending any extra writing or voice budget to produce that second reaction.","compare":"Dark Souls’ Lordran stages a comparably decayed kingdom almost entirely through item descriptions and architecture, but no player has ever walked through Lordran intact in an earlier game, so its ruin can only ever be read cold, without the specific, personal comparison Breath of the Wild offers its own veterans.","cost":"This layered effect only reaches players who own the series’ history, so it cannot be the primary way the game communicates its backstory to newcomers, who need the same ruins to work on their own, unassisted terms as ordinary environmental storytelling.","principle":"A series with enough entries can stage the same location in both a healthy and a ruined state across different games and let the comparison itself carry meaning, but only as a bonus layer for returning players, never as a substitute for staging the ruin so it also works on a first visit.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time"]},"business":{"claim":"Breath of the Wild launched simultaneously on the Wii U it was originally built for and the brand-new Nintendo Switch on 3 March 2017, hedging the new console’s uncertain launch instead of making the flagship game exclusive to it.","evidence":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild released the same day, worldwide, as both a Wii U title and the Nintendo Switch’s own launch game, making it the last major first-party release for the Wii U and the first for the Switch at once.","mechanism":"Splitting a single flagship release across an outgoing and an incoming console lets a publisher recover a full development budget from the installed base of the old platform regardless of how the new one performs, while still giving the new console a system-selling exclusive-in-spirit title on day one.","effect":"Wii U owners who had waited years through the console’s commercial failure got a genuine ending to that platform’s library, while Switch buyers got proof, on day one, that the new hardware could run the exact game already being called the series’ best.","compare":"Halo: Combat Evolved (2001) took the opposite bet as the Xbox’s own launch showcase, released only for the brand-new console at launch, after Microsoft bought Bungie and moved it off Mac and PC, with a PC version following in 2003, staking the whole game’s audience on the new hardware’s success.","cost":"Supporting two consoles at once meant Breath of the Wild had to run on the Wii U’s far weaker hardware too, and reviewers and players who compared the two versions noted the Wii U release ran below the docked Switch version.","principle":"When a new console’s early sales are uncertain, releasing the generation’s flagship game on the outgoing platform as well as the new one can recover its cost either way, at the price of that flagship running at its weakest on the console it was actually built for.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Breath_of_the_Wild","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halo:_Combat_Evolved"]},"replay":{"claim":"Ocarina of Time’s Master Quest, first released in Japan in 2002 on a GameCube bonus disc, sells a second playthrough of the same game by rewriting only its dungeons, and the 2011 3DS remake stacked a mirrored world and double damage on top of those same redesigned dungeons.","evidence":"Master Quest began as “Ura Zelda” for the Nintendo 64DD and reached players on a GameCube bonus disc bundled with preorders of The Wind Waker in Japan (2002) and North America (2003), keeping Ocarina of Time’s engine and plot while altering its dungeons and puzzles and raising their difficulty. Ocarina of Time 3D (2011) kept those redesigned dungeons in its own Master Quest and added two changes: the whole world is flipped left to right, and every enemy deals double damage.","mechanism":"The remix works because it targets only the part of the game players solve rather than the parts they travel through or watch: a veteran’s knowledge of the story, the overworld and every item stays valid, while the memorised answers to each dungeon, where the key is and which switch opens which door, no longer apply.","effect":"A returning player walks familiar fields to a familiar dungeon entrance and then finds the rooms asking new questions, so the second run tests reasoning again instead of recall; the 3DS mirror adds a milder layer on top, making even the unchanged overworld briefly feel unfamiliar.","compare":"Mario Kart’s Mirror Mode, present since Mario Kart 64 (1996), flips its tracks horizontally without changing any corner, which tests memory of a route rather than posing a new problem; Ocarina of Time 3D uses the same flip, but only as garnish on dungeons whose puzzles were rewritten underneath it.","cost":"Rewriting every dungeon costs level-design time for content only a fraction of owners play, and Master Quest first shipped only as a preorder bonus rather than a product of its own; the 3DS version’s mirror and damage multiplier, by contrast, cost almost nothing to add because they change no room at all.","principle":"A replay mode earns its place when it changes the questions rather than the stats: rearranging where the puzzles and enemies sit forces veterans to reason again, where a damage multiplier alone only asks them to repeat the same answers more carefully.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time_Master_Quest","https://www.nintendolife.com/news/2011/05/you_must_finish_ocarina_of_time_3d_to_take_on_the_master_quest"]},"lineage":{"claim":"Ocarina of Time’s Z-targeting, which locks the camera and Link’s facing onto one enemy or object at the press of a button, is credited with popularising the target-lock system that has since become common in 3D action and adventure games.","evidence":"With Z-targeting on, “the camera follows the target and Link constantly faces it,” so sideways movement becomes a circle-strafe and projectiles fly at the target automatically. Nintendo’s staff traced the idea to a field trip: at a ninja show at Toei Kyoto Studio Park, Yoshiaki Koizumi noticed that the ninjas surrounding the hero attacked one at a time while the rest waited, a staging he later called a clue toward solving 3D combat.","mechanism":"Z-targeting works as a constraint that removes a degree of freedom, the camera’s independent aim, in exchange for legibility: once locked, the game can safely assume the player is facing their target, letting designers build combat encounters and puzzles around a guaranteed facing direction rather than an unpredictable one.","effect":"A convention players now expect from any third-person action game, that a shoulder button will fix their view on the thing that matters, traces back to a stage fight’s choreography adapted for a Nintendo 64 camera, not to any later, better-funded 3D engine.","compare":"Core Design’s Tomb Raider (1996) already locked Lara’s pistols onto nearby enemies automatically whenever her weapons were drawn, so a lock-on as such was not new in 1998; what Z-targeting added was a single target the player chooses, around which Link’s movement, facing and the camera all reorganise themselves.","cost":"Fixing the camera onto one target necessarily narrows a player’s view of everything else in the room, which is why most lock-on systems, Ocarina of Time’s included, let a player release the lock instantly, trading the assist away the moment situational awareness matters more than precision on one target.","principle":"When a new dimension breaks a genre’s old answers, look for the problem already solved in another medium: stage-fight choreography, one attacker engaging while the rest wait their turn, handed Nintendo both a camera rule and an enemy-behaviour rule at once.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_Ocarina_of_Time","https://www.nintendo.com/en-gb/Iwata-Asks/Iwata-Asks-The-Legend-of-Zelda-Ocarina-of-Time-3D/Vol-2-Original-Development-Staff-Part-1/3-Let-s-Go-to-Toei-Kyoto-Studio-Park-/3-Let-s-Go-to-Toei-Kyoto-Studio-Park--231680.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_Raider_(1996_video_game)"]}},"shots":[{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-climb.webp","lens":"gameplay","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild: Link, shield on his back, glides down a sandstone canyon under his paraglider, past a giant carved statue.","caption":"Nothing here is item-gated: the paraglider Link carries from the opening plateau turns any drop into a route.","callouts":[{"x":0.4,"y":0.2,"t":"Paraglider: given on the first plateau, not found in a dungeon"},{"x":0.66,"y":0.52,"t":"The ruin ahead has no locked door to open first"}],"credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-breath-of-the-wild-switch/","licence":"store"}},{"img":"assets/games/shots/zelda-ztarget.webp","lens":"lineage","alt":"The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (2026 Nintendo Switch 2 remake): Link fights two Stalfos skeleton warriors, pink triangular Z-target reticles marking the locked-on enemy.","caption":"The pink triangles are Z-targeting’s reticle: lock on, and the camera and Link’s facing both follow this one enemy.","callouts":[{"x":0.52,"y":0.36,"t":"Z-target reticle: locks the camera on this enemy"},{"x":0.46,"y":0.8,"t":"Link keeps facing his target while locked on"}],"credit":{"author":"Nintendo","url":"https://www.nintendo.com/us/store/products/the-legend-of-zelda-ocarina-of-time-switch-2/","licence":"store"}}]},"519c3102");
